NAHPU supports three [field workflows](/en/usages/workflows/), and this series walks through the two
designed for multiple catalogers working across multiple devices:

- **[Workflow A](/en/usages/workflows/#workflow-a): shared site, parallel
  catalogs.** Specimens come from a shared site and collecting event (e.g., a
  mist-net check, a pitfall line, or a Malaise trap), and each person catalogs
  the specimens they sort on their own device.
- **[Workflow B](/en/usages/workflows/#workflow-b): separate sites, parallel
  catalogs.** Each person runs their own trap line, then prepares and catalogs
  its catch independently.

In both workflows, everyone shares the same project and personnel records,
keeps a separate specimen catalog, and merges those catalogs into one project
at the end. Real field teams often combine both approaches, and this series
does the same.

## The scenario

Two teammates work the 2014 Mt. Tujuh survey from the
[Basic tutorials](/en/tutorials/basic/paper-catalog/):

- **Heru** (project lead) creates the project, personnel, taxa, and shared
  Line 1 sites and events.
- **Bima** (teammate) copies the shared setup onto his own device by scanning
  QR codes and importing event files.
- **Workflow A:** Both catalog the morning check of Line 1 on 13 September 2014
  in parallel (i.e., Heru records the snap-trap catch, and Bima records the
  pitfall catch).
- **Workflow B:** Bima also runs Line 2 on his own and catalogs its catch
  independently.
- **Merge and export:** Heru merges Bima's catalog into the main project,
  checks every tissue into one freezer box, and exports a single Darwin Core
  Archive.

{% aside type="note" title="A re-enactment of real records" %}
In 2014, Heru cataloged every specimen himself. These tutorials imagine that
Bima Sampel (a fictional teammate) cataloged the pitfall catch and ran Line 2.
Bima's five records are real specimens from the paper catalog (HH 451 to HH 454
and HH 456), renumbered `BS1` to `BS5` for teaching. All measurements, dates,
and tissue numbers match the original paper forms.
{% /aside %}

## How to practice

Before starting, work through
[Basic tutorials 2–5](/en/tutorials/basic/project-personnel-taxa/), which
introduce each form used here. Allow about an hour for the series.

- **With lab members (recommended):** Practice as a pair, or as a small lab
  group, during a lab meeting or pre-field prep session, with each person on
  their own phone, tablet, or laptop. One person takes **Heru's role** (project
  lead) and a teammate takes **Bima's role**.
- **With three or more lab members:** Extra teammates can pair up on a role or
  join the same project as additional catalogers. Add each extra person with
  the `Cataloger` role and their own initials in Step 2 (e.g., promoting
  `Citra Contoh` to `Cataloger` or using your own names), have them follow
  Bima's steps, and merge each catalog into the lead's device at the end.
- **Solo on two devices:** You can also practice on your own using two NAHPU
  installations (e.g., a tablet and a phone, or a laptop and a phone), playing
  both roles yourself.

Any combination of devices works. The screenshots show Heru on an iPad and Bima
on an iPhone, but the steps are identical on Android, iOS, macOS, Windows,
Linux, and the web. QR code scanning works on devices with a camera (Android,
iOS, macOS, and the web); on devices without a camera, use the JSON file export
and import shown in
[Teamwork tutorial 3](/en/tutorials/teamwork/share-events-and-taxa/).

